Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Final final call for participation:

This year, there will be three bootcamps:

- a 4-day bootcamp on statistics for linguists with R (starting from relatively scratch but then aiming to focus on regression modeling) at the University of Mississippi, Oxford (4-7 August 2014).
- a 5-day bootcamp on corpus linguistics with R (frequency lists, concordancing, etc.) at UCSB (11-15 or 16 August 2014);
- a 5-day bootcamp on statistics for linguistics with R (introduction, mono- and multifactorial tests, and regression modeling) at UCSB (18-22 or 23 August 2014).

There are still some places available in all bootcamps so

- if you're interested in the Mississippi bootcamp, plz contact Chris Sapp (<http://home.olemiss.edu/~csapp/ <http://home.olemiss.edu/%7Ecsapp/>>);
- if you're interested in one or both of the UCSB bootcamps, plz contact Stefanie Wulff (<http://www.clas.ufl.edu/users/swulff/>).

From ue1b933a0d5bbe14f at groups.google.com  Fri May 30 02:21:15 2014
From: ue1b933a0d5bbe14f at groups.google.com (Kevin Parent)
Date: Fri, 30 May 2014 02:21:15 +0000
Subject: appending files in Windows 8
Message-ID: <ISsrKM4ZcNQ.54Z7MrLFn5EJ@corpling-with-r.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/corpling-with-r/c/ISsrKM4ZcNQ/m/54Z7MrLFn5EJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I'm running what should be a straight-forward script on R running on Windows 8, but I keep hitting problems when I write the data to a file. The end of the script looks more or less like this:

cat(file='file.csv',output.column.names)
for(x in 1:length(output.data)){
cat(file='file.csv',output.data[x],sep='\n',append=T)
}

The problem is not just that I keep getting 'Permission denied' errors that crash the script, but that it crashes at different places. Sometimes it outputs some 300 lines before the error occurs. Other times x exceeds 1000. But it's always a different number.

So here's the workaround. To get it to work, I have to add:
for(delay in 1:300000){
}
before the looped cat command. Apparently R tries to access the file to write before the OS has really closed it. The 300,000 is the result of several trial-and-error runs.

So I got it to work, but I'm curious if anyone knows a better way to deal with this rather ridiculous problem. (Other than running R in Linux as I usually do.)

--
Kevin Parent, Ph.D
Korea Maritime University

